^^Skill Notes^^

Each Burmese consonant has creaky “ a ” tone. For example, က (ka) , ခ (kha) etc., 

^Tones^

Burmese has 3-4 tones and Akari made a latin script based on IPA (International Phonetic Alphabets)

- creaky tone will be spelt normally 
- *grave accent ( ` )* for *low tones*
- *acute accent ( ´ )* for *high tones*
-  t is added at the end for *checked tones*

^Romanization^

Burmese doesn't have an official romanization. The one that locals use is based on English spellings.

_For example,_
“ Hoke ” (yes) -oke as in broke

  “ -r ” is used for low/high tones as in Myanmar. Spoiler alert : the r there is just a tone marker, it is not pronounced.

“ -oe ” is used for high o tone , “ t ” is used as creaky tone marker in some words.
